Ladybird gives each browser tab and window an integer id from a
per-process counter, kept on the frontend’s own tab and window objects,
so a tab keeps its id across navigations, process swaps, and moves
between windows, and no id is reused. Chromium does the same with
SessionID: ExtensionTabUtil::GetTabId() returns
SessionTabHelper::IdForTab(), a per-session id that stays with the
WebContents. Gecko’s TabTracker::getId() hands out ids from its own
_nextId counter, once per native tab, and keeps them when a tab moves to
another window. WebKit leaves tabs and windows to the embedding
application, which supplies them through its WebExtensionTab and
WebExtensionWindow objects.

Ladybird enumerates the frontend’s tabs when an extension asks, rather
than keeping a registry the frontends feed, since a tab restored from a
session has no view until it’s shown. Chromium and Gecko enumerate their
live tabs on demand too: Chromium’s TabsQueryFunction::Run() walks each
browser’s tab strip model, and Gecko’s TabManagerBase.query() walks the
windows its window tracker knows.
